{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>But on the tenth day of this seventh month</b> — all the אך and רק in the Torah are restrictive (Rosh Hashanah 17a, Sanhedrin 49b): it atones for those who repent but does not atone for those who do not repent — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:27), from the words of our Sages (Sifra ad loc.; Shevuot 13a). If so, the meaning of the verse would be: \"On the first day of the month you shall have a day of remembrance of teru'ah for all of you\" — that you shall all be judged before Him — \"but for some of you, on the tenth of the month, it shall be the Day of Atonements.\" This is the sense of בלבד (\"only / exclusively\"), as in \"Please bear my sin only this once\" (Exodus 10:17), and similarly \"Has He spoken only with Moses?\" (Numbers 12:2) — the exclusivity is with Moses alone. And so too, \"But on the fifteenth day of the seventh month\" (Leviticus 23:39): he says, only on the fifteenth shall you celebrate the festival of YHWH for seven days, not consecutively, because the festival offering does not override Shabbat. And we shall interpret it in this manner for all the commandments according to the tradition of our Sages. And similar to it: \"But he was surely torn\" (Genesis 44:28) — that nothing else happened to him apart from the tearing. And likewise: \"For the children of Israel and the children of Judah have only been doing what is evil in My eyes from their youth, for the children of Israel have only been provoking Me\" (Jeremiah 32:30) — that they did nothing else. According to the plain sense, אך means \"surely / indeed,\" affirming the truth of the matter: \"Surely the thing is known\" (Exodus 12:14), \"Surely you shall die like men\" (Psalms 82:7). \"Surely on the tenth of the month it is the Day of Atonements\" — an affirmation of the truth of the matter; he says: On the first of the month is the day of judgment, but assuredly on the tenth of the month is the Day of Atonements — therefore you shall afflict your souls and shall do no work. And likewise: \"You are truly my bone and my flesh\" (Genesis 29:14), \"He is truly the king of Israel\" (I Kings 22:32) [n: Hebrew text here reads מלכים א כב לב = I Kings 22:32], \"Truly El is good to Israel, to those of pure heart\" (Psalms 73:1). And similarly the interpretation of \"But My Sabbaths you shall keep\" (Exodus 31:12): I have commanded you concerning the work of the Tabernacle, but indeed My Sabbaths you shall keep forever. And all of them will be interpreted for you in this manner if you exercise insight in them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:44.538929865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}